    "I’ll read the project instructions and OpenSocrates controller, then build the app and verify its API, browser workflow, and persistence.",
    "I’m using the installed OpenSocrates controller for the main design decisions. The project is empty, so I’m choosing Python’s standard library, SQLite, and plain JavaScript to keep setup local and reproducible.",
    "The critical boundary is one SQLite write transaction per mutation. It will check the idempotency key, validate the current version and stock, write the order and inventory changes, append one audit event, and store the replay response before committing.",
    "The API and UI are in place. I’m moving to route-level and browser checks now; the first syntax pass caught a server typo, which I fixed before starting the app.",
    "Route tests pass for the main workflow, cross-tenant access, role limits, failed reservation rollback, concurrent reservations, replay, returns, pagination, and restart. I’m checking the same flow in Chromium now, including the form controls and data refresh after each action.",
    "The mobile Chromium workflow passes: sign in, create a two-line order, reserve, ship, return it fully, inspect audit and inventory, then sign in as a viewer and confirm write controls are absent. I found one test locator that missed visible status text; the workflow itself completed, and the revised assertion passes.",
